d":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE5MDg5NTI0Mzk="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1539769432,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDU2MjYxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-430562617"},"message":"Re normpath:\nAgree, that's an issue. It was introduced to have a canonical form to compare paths (for common prefix for example). And for that, it's kind of the right thing, but only after whatever resolution strategy we come up with regarding the more general issue. Does pathlib provide something similar to `os.normpath`? May be we don't need it at all, when we switch?\n\nRe general symlink resolution:\nNeed to think about it. Solution isn't obvious to me.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539769921,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDU2NTI0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-430565244"},"message":"pathlib does not have normpath or anything like it, because what normpath does is simply not valid for an arbitrary POSIX path that may or may not contain symlinks to directories (pathlib docs call this functionality \"a naïve approach\").\n\nAt the very least we would have to have specific normpath implementation that does NOT remove any `..` if any of the components is a symlink to a directory. But keep in mind: in the general case, we will not know whether this is the case (nothing yet on the filesystem), hence, in general, we cannot do what normpath does.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539775089,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDU4OTA2OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-430589069"},"message":"More fun: I just learned that even on Windows (without any symlinks involved) we might need to resolve input paths, as they might come in in \"DOS path\" form, and need to become a full windows path in order to have our machinery working:\n\nThese guys:\n\n`C:\\PROGRA~1` -\u003e `C:\\Program Files`\n\nLife is a bitch.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539778506,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE5MDkzNTgzMDg="},"added":["! important !"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1539796745,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDcxMjExMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-430712113"},"message":"Re windows: There has to be some (windows only) package dealing with that. It actually is not like a symlink (FS feature), but a different (older) windows API, that reports those 8.3 char paths for backwards compatibility. However, finding such a package or tool may enable us to deal with it like a symlink. If I'm not mistaken we could detect such a thing by its containing `~` and then resolve it, following the same strategy. Yes - we don't have a strategy yet, but at least it possibly can be treated the same way instead of raising an additional and different issue.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1539796745,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE0MTU2ODMyNg=="},"target":"46a08028cfd8fc84299e8228045d3341261467ce5ac60df62f338a37213e293f","message":"Re windows: There has to be some (windows only) package dealing with that. It actually is not like a symlink (FS feature), but a different (older) windows API, that reports those 8.3 char paths for backwards compatibility. However, finding such a package or tool may enable us to deal with it like a symlink. If I'm not mistaken we could detect such a thing by its containing `~` and then resolve it, following the same strategy. Yes - we don't have a strategy yet, but at least it possibly can be treated the same way instead of raising an additional and different issue.\n\n\nEdit:\nThis might help us with windows:\nhttps://stackoverflow.com/questions/6085688/getting-short-path-in-python","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539838187,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDg3NDI0Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-430874242"},"message":"yet to fully comprehend/analyze so pardon if this comment has 0 value, but regarding `normpath`:\n\n\u003e Regardless of whether using absolute paths or relative ones the outcome is always wrong:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e In [2]: op.normpath('link/../dir2/file')\n\u003e Out[2]: 'dir2/file'\n\u003e \n\u003e In [3]: op.normpath('/tmp/this/link/../dir2/file')\n\u003e Out[3]: '/tmp/this/dir2/file'\n\u003e ```\n\noutput sounds actually \"correct\" to me, since `normpath` does not (and should not) resolve any symlinks, neither test for existence of files etc.  If you need some other function, which does some clever symlink resolution, it would be something else but `normpath`.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539838521,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMDg3NTAwNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-430875006"},"message":"The output of normpath is correct, no question. But the output path no longer points to the user-intended location, and THAT is what causes some of the grief we are seeing in datalad operations.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539868166,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMTAwMTkxMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-431001910"},"message":"\u003e ... But the output path no longer points to the user-intended location...\n\nTo me, as the user of `normpath` it points exactly to the location I intend it to point.\nIndeed though, if the intention is to dereference symlinked directories while at it (e.g. for the purpose of identifying the dataset containing the symlinked location), then it would be the \"incorrect\" location, but such job is not of `normpath`. So indeed it smells like we need some additional helper (e.g. `dereferenced_normpath`) which would not simply operate on path strings without considering current state of the filesystem, but probably perform the checks/logic you have described.  Note that because it would need to consult the filesystem it would require corresponding (sub-)datasets to be installed, and probably must fail if any component of the path is not there... I wonder if there would be some intended uses where such actions would not be appropriate (e.g. normalizing the path for a perspective dataset/file).\nHaving that in mind, related 1c -- I fear that symlinks across datasets is asking for a trouble.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539878104,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMTA2MzY2OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-431063669"},"message":"I think we should focus on datalad users not normpath users. So are you saying that my example above is something that datalad should not support? That would simplify path handling quite a bit-- but it would also impact actual usage.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539881290,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMTA4MTgzNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-431081837"},"message":"\u003e So are you saying that my example above is something that datalad should not support? \n\nno","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539939511,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMTI5NDg2NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-431294864"},"message":"We need to come up with a definitive rule, otherwise it is impossible to even decide what a real bug looks like. For example #2885 is a real problem IMHO. Any solution should be derived from some sort of \nfundational rule.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3d4093ba1852d0599c7b70904b94d21d5cf02d05"},"timestamp":1539978995,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMTQ4MTMwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-431481308"},"message":"not-sure-if-relevant sidenote: git-annex commands don't follow symlinks:\nhttps://git-annex.branchable.com/todo/symlinks_to_symlinks_to_the_annex/\nmaybe you can help me convince joey that they should :)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1540000250,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMTUzOTI4Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-431539283"},"message":"\u003e ... it is impossible to even decide what a real bug looks like. \n\nhttps://youtu.be/EHX7NZS8zAI\nSorry I don't know if there is CC","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540011136,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMTU0ODY3NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-431548674"},"message":"@notestaff thx this ia very helpful!","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540011136,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE0MjY1MDE0OQ=="},"target":"9f480cfeb05cfe99779f28cde5e218291232318f66a93b947af4b27525c62844","message":"@notestaff thx this is very helpful!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540371239,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMjU3MTgyOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-432571828"},"message":"FTR: https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/pull/28 now documents the behavior re symlinks in -revolution. Bottom line: a symlink is a symlink (and just that), unless it is used as a placeholder for an annexed file (by git annex itself, in locked modes). In the latter case it is treated as if it would be the actual file (reported as type 'file').\n\nSo far that seems to scale across platforms and modes -- but this is probably not the end of the journey...","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540745945,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzcyMjUwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2928#issuecomment-433722501"},"message":"I will close this for now. When I have used the current implementation in practice for a while, I will reevaluate this.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540745945,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTkzMDg2Mzc4MQ=="},"status":2}]}